.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< typename > class operation>
.br
class csmp::ConstantFactor< dim, operation >"
.PP
\fBAuthor\fP
.RS 4
S\&.K\&. Matthaei 

.PP
S\&. Geiger 

.PP
S\&. Roberts 
.RE
.PP
\fBDate\fP
.RS 4
2002
.RE
.PP
\fBConstantFactor\fP is an interrelation which allows users to combine an arbitrary number of variables\&. An STL binary function object is used to define the operation used to combine the variables\&. For most applications, std::multiplies (for multiplication) and std::plus (for summation) of the STL base library will be used, but in principle one is free to define an arbitrary binary function\&.
.SH "Applicability"
.PP
Use this interrelation when you need to combine different variables\&.
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
The implementation makes use of the STL binary function classes and the \fBOperand\fP class\&. Therefore, combining element and nodal variables should be possible (not tested)\&. The first operand is used to initialize the result operand, which results in the following behaviour when using non-commutative operands (here shown for std::minus):

.PP
.PP
.nf
result_property = first_property \- (\\sum other_properties)\&.
.fi
.PP
 
